          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Sources: <a href="https://pbsports.punjab.gov.in/reservation-of-sportsmen.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Punjab sports department</a>, a <a href="https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/haryana/group-c-posts-3-quota-for-outstanding-sportspersons-632336" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tribune report on Haryana</a> (June 2024), and state orders and news reports for the others. Rules change, so read the current recruitment notice.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--gold">
    <p>
      If you aim for a government post, build one parallel route at the same time: an after-school coaching group, a data skill, or a fitness certification. A parallel skill also gives you a fallback if the notice is withdrawn or the vacancy count is small. For the wider comparison, see <a href="/blog/government-jobs/govt-vs-private-job-india/">government versus private jobs in India</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="money">What the B.P.Ed should cost you</h2>

  <p>
    Here the degree really matters, because the school PE post usually requires it. That is a good reason to get one. It is not a reason to overspend. College is a small part of career success, and the course is a gate, not the whole plan.
  </p>

  <p>
    A starting rule: spend about 10% of your complete education budget on college. If you spend above roughly 20%, you need course-specific evidence. This is a planning heuristic, not a law.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Reported 2-year cost</th>
          <th>Against an example budget</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {moneyRows.map((item) => (
