            <td data-label="Reported 2-year cost">{item.cost}</td>
            <td data-label="Against an example budget">{item.share}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Fee ranges come from community answers on Careers360, not official fee schedules, and the Rs 6 lakh budget is only an example. Read each college's current prospectus.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A private B.P.Ed can be justified only if the college is NCTE-recognised and can show where its graduates actually work, with how many placed out of how many in the batch. If it cannot, a government or university department seat through the entrance route is usually the better use of money. Keep the rest for first aid and coaching certificates, equipment, a laptop, video tools and your first academy batch. Avoid education debt for this course unless a seat is the only way to the credential.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Much of coaching knowledge is free or cheap: federation coaching courses, university lectures, and YouTube for analysis tools. Judge each course by who teaches it, how recent it is, and whether you finish with something you can show. A pile of certificates is not a skill portfolio, and none of it replaces the B.P.Ed where a post requires it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sales">Selling coaching: who pays and why they trust you</h2>

  <p>
    Nobody teaches this in the course, and it decides whether a coach earns a salary or builds a business. Keep it simple, and promise no income.
  </p>

